timeline('Sikkim|West Bengal',
    ('by 1814', 'Gorkha (Nepali) forces expanding from the west overrun Sikkim as far east as the Teesta river and annex its entire Terai.', ['h_sikkimkingdom']),
    ('1815-16', 'The British defeat Nepal in the Anglo-Gorkha War; by the Treaty of Sugauli, Nepal cedes the Terai it had taken, including the tract between the Mechi and the Teesta.', ['sugauli', 'h_sikkimkingdom']),
    ('10 Feb 1817', 'The Treaty of Titalia restores the land between the Mechi and the Teesta to Sikkim rather than keeping it under direct British rule, recreating Sikkim as a state with a southern frontier in the plains.', ['h_titalia']),
    ('1 Feb 1835', 'The Chogyal of Sikkim grants the Darjeeling tract, about 138 square miles, to the East India Company as a hill sanatorium, in exchange for an annual payment.', ['h_darjeelinggov', 'h_darjeelingwiki']),
    ('1849-Feb 1850', 'After Sikkim briefly detains British officials Archibald Campbell and Joseph Hooker in 1849, Britain retaliates by annexing about 640 square miles of the Sikkim Terai and adjoining hill country between the Rangit river and the plains, and stops its annual payment to the Chogyal.', ['h_darjeelinggov', 'h_darjeelingwiki']),
    ('28 Mar 1861', 'After further fighting, the Treaty of Tumlong ends the conflict and makes Sikkim a British protectorate in practice, stabilising its southern frontier with British Bengal.', ['h_tumlong', 'h_sikkimkingdom']),
    ('Nov 1864 / 11 Nov 1865', 'The Treaty of Sinchula ends the Duar War: Bhutan cedes the Bengal Duars and the Kalimpong hill tract to British India; sources differ on whether the treaty is dated November 1864 or signed 11 November 1865.', ['h_sinchula', 'h_darjeelinggov']),
    ('1866', 'Kalimpong is added to Darjeeling district, which reaches its present area of about 1,234 square miles; its northern edge against Sikkim is now fixed.', ['h_darjeelinggov']),
    ('5 Dec 1950', 'The Indo-Sikkimese Treaty makes Sikkim a protectorate of India, with India handling defence, external affairs and communications; the 19th-century cession line becomes, in practice, an internal Indian administrative boundary.', ['h_sikkimkingdom']),
    ('14 Apr - 16 May 1975', 'A referendum abolishes the monarchy; the 36th Amendment makes Sikkim the 22nd state of India on 16 May 1975, turning the old Sikkim-Bengal frontier into an inter-state border.', ['h_sikkimmerger']),
)

timeline('Nepal|West Bengal',
    ('18th c.', 'Gorkha forces expanding east from Nepal overrun Sikkim’s Terai as far as the Teesta river, so Nepal comes to directly border the tract that will later become Darjeeling district.', ['h_sikkimkingdom']),
    ('1815-16', 'After the Anglo-Gorkha War, the Treaty of Sugauli forces Nepal to renounce the Terai lands it had conquered; the Mechi river becomes Nepal’s eastern limit in this sector, with the land between the Mechi and the Teesta passing to the East India Company.', ['sugauli']),
    ('10 Feb 1817', 'The Treaty of Titalia restores the Mechi-Teesta tract to Sikkim rather than retaining it, so the Mechi becomes the line between Nepal and Sikkim, not Nepal and British territory, opposite the later Darjeeling.', ['h_titalia']),
    ('1835 &amp; 1849-50', 'Sikkim cedes the Darjeeling tract (1835) and then the Sikkim Terai (1849-50) to the British, so British Indian territory comes to face Nepal directly along the Mechi in this sector.', ['h_darjeelinggov']),
    ('15 Aug 1947', 'The partition of Bengal creates the Indian state of West Bengal; Darjeeling district, whose Mechi frontier with Nepal is not itself divided by the Radcliffe award, becomes part of it.', ['m13', 'h_westbengal']),
    ('1981-2007', 'A joint technical committee maps the India-Nepal boundary; strip maps cover about 98% of its length, and this sector is not among the areas left in dispute.', ['stripmaps']),
)

timeline('Nepal|Sikkim',
    ('pre-1774', 'The Limbu hill chiefs between Kangchenjunga and the Teesta river come under the Bhutia kings of Sikkim, giving Sikkim’s original western frontier with the Limbu country of eastern Nepal.', ['h_limbuwan']),
    ('1771-74', 'The Gorkha kingdom conquers Limbuwan, including Ilam, under Prithvi Narayan Shah; by 1775 Gorkha forces are already pushing further east into Sikkim through Ilam.', ['h_limbuwan']),
    ('by 1814', 'Continued Gorkha expansion overruns Sikkim as far as the Teesta river, erasing the old Limbuwan-Sikkim frontier entirely.', ['h_sikkimkingdom']),
    ('1815-16', 'The Anglo-Gorkha War ends in the Treaty of Sugauli, by which Nepal renounces the territory it had conquered east of the Mechi river.', ['sugauli']),
    ('10 Feb 1817', 'The Treaty of Titalia restores the tract between the Mechi and the Teesta to Sikkim, re-establishing a Sikkim-Nepal frontier close to its pre-Gorkha line, running up the ridge toward Kangchenjunga.', ['h_titalia']),
    ('19th-20th c.', 'The frontier settles along the Singalila Ridge, the watershed dividing Sikkim and Darjeeling from Ilam and Taplejung districts of Nepal, reaching Kangchenjunga at the Nepal-India-China trijunction.', ['h_singalila', 'h_kangchenjunga']),
    ('14 Apr - 16 May 1975', 'Sikkim’s merger with India turns this from a frontier of a British-protected Himalayan state into the boundary between Nepal and an Indian state.', ['h_sikkimmerger']),
)

timeline('Bhutan|West Bengal',
    ('c. 1730-1772', 'Bhutan holds suzerainty over the plains kingdom of Cooch Behar and the adjoining Duars, giving it a long frontier in the foothills.', ['h_bhutanindiaborder']),
    ('1772-73', 'A disputed Cooch Behar succession draws in the East India Company, which occupies Cooch Behar and skirmishes with Bhutanese forces in the adjoining duars.', ['h_bhutanindiaborder']),
    ('25 Apr 1774', 'The Anglo-Bhutanese Treaty ends the fighting: Bhutan withdraws to roughly its pre-1730 limits, and Cooch Behar remains under British protection, an early version of the plains frontier.', ['h_anglobhutan1774']),
    ('1841-42', 'Continuing friction over the duars leads Britain to annex the Bengal and Assam Duars for a time, a foretaste of the permanent cession that follows in 1865.', ['h_bhutanindiaborder']),
    ('1864-65', 'The Duar War ends with the Treaty of Sinchula, signed 11 November 1865: Bhutan permanently cedes the Bengal and Assam Duars and part of Dewangiri, about 2,000 square miles in all, to British India for an annual subsidy.', ['h_sinchula', 'h_duarwar']),
    ('after 1865', 'The ceded Bengal Duars are organised into Jalpaiguri district; Alipurduar, on the Bhutan frontier, later splits off as a separate district on 25 June 2014.', ['h_alipurduar']),
    ('8 Jan 1910', 'The Treaty of Punakha doubles Bhutan’s subsidy and gives Britain a guiding role in its external relations, without changing the 1865 boundary.', ['h_punakha1910']),
    ('15 Aug 1947', 'West Bengal is formed at partition and inherits Bengal’s frontier with Bhutan along the former Duars.', ['h_westbengal']),
    ('8 Aug 1949', 'The Treaty of Perpetual Peace and Friendship, signed at Darjeeling, has independent India take over Britain’s role guiding Bhutan’s external relations; the boundary carries over unchanged.', ['h_indobhutan1949']),
    ('1961-2006', 'India and Bhutan survey and demarcate their boundary; final maps are signed at Thimphu in December 2006, fixing the modern West Bengal-Bhutan line with pillars.', ['h_bhutandemarcation']),
)

timeline('Bhutan|Sikkim',
    ('from 1642', 'By Sikkimese tradition, the kingdom founded in 1642 includes the Chumbi valley and the Haa valley around the Doklam plateau, so Sikkim and Bhutan originally meet further east than today.', ['h_doklam', 'h_chumbivalley']),
    ('after 1780', 'Following a Bhutanese attack, a settlement transfers the Haa valley, including the Doklam plateau, and the Kalimpong area from Sikkim to Bhutan, moving Sikkim’s eastern frontier west.', ['h_doklam']),
    ('1792', 'After the Sino-Nepalese War, a Chinese survey declares the Chumbi valley part of Tibet, detaching it from Sikkim and leaving Sikkim’s northern frontier with Bhutan bounded by Tibetan territory to the north.', ['h_chumbivalley']),
    ('28 Mar 1861', 'The Treaty of Tumlong makes Sikkim a British protectorate, bringing this frontier under British oversight along with the rest of Sikkim’s borders.', ['h_tumlong']),
    ('17 Mar 1890', 'The Anglo-Chinese Convention on Sikkim and Tibet defines the Sikkim-Tibet boundary as the watershed between the Teesta and the Mochu rivers, starting at Mount Gipmochi; no map is attached, leaving the exact position of the Sikkim-Bhutan-Tibet trijunction, near Doklam, ambiguous.', ['m35', 'h_doklam']),
    ('1907-13', 'British survey maps place the trijunction not at Gipmochi itself but further north at Batang La, on the higher Batang La-Merug La-Sinchela ridge; this Gipmochi-Batang La ambiguity underlies the later Doklam dispute.', ['h_doklam']),
    ('14 Apr - 16 May 1975', 'Sikkim’s merger with India makes this the Bhutan-India (Sikkim) boundary, with the trijunction question still unresolved.', ['h_sikkimmerger']),
    ('Jun-Aug 2017', 'Chinese road-building on the Doklam plateau, claimed by Bhutan, prompts Indian troops to cross from Sikkim into the area in Bhutan’s support; the 73-day standoff ends on 28 August 2017 with mutual disengagement, leaving the trijunction unsettled.', ['h_doklam']),
)

timeline('China|Sikkim',
    ('28 Mar 1861', 'The Treaty of Tumlong makes Sikkim a British protectorate, giving Britain an interest in fixing Sikkim’s undefined northern frontier with Tibet.', ['h_tumlong']),
    ('17 Mar 1890', 'The Anglo-Chinese Convention of Calcutta, between Britain and Qing China, defines the Sikkim-Tibet boundary as the watershed between the Teesta and the Mochu rivers, running from Mount Gipmochi on the Bhutan frontier to the Nepal frontier, and China recognises the British protectorate over Sikkim.', ['m35', 'h_calcutta1890']),
    ('1895', 'Britain and China jointly demarcate this line on the ground; it is the only stretch of today’s China-India frontier fixed by a treaty both sides have accepted.', ['h_calcutta1890']),
    ('1950', 'China’s takeover of Tibet brings the People’s Republic of China to this frontier for the first time, in place of the Tibetan and Qing authorities of before.', ['h_calcutta1890']),
    ('11-14 Sep and 1 Oct 1967', 'Chinese and Indian troops clash at the Nathu La and Cho La passes after disputes over fencing and patrolling; Indian forces hold their posts in both actions.', ['h_nathula1967']),
    ('14 Apr - 16 May 1975', 'Sikkim’s merger with India makes this the China-India border in the Sikkim sector, though China continues for decades to treat Sikkim’s status as unsettled.', ['h_sikkimmerger']),
    ('23 Jun 2003', 'India and China sign a declaration under which India recognises the Tibet Autonomous Region as part of China and China implicitly accepts Sikkim as part of India, also agreeing to reopen Nathu La for border trade.', ['h_nathula1967']),
    ('2005-06', 'A joint statement declares that Sikkim is no longer an issue in India-China relations, and Nathu La reopens for border trade in 2006, the first crossing to do so since 1962.', ['h_nathula1967']),
    ('Jun-Aug 2017', 'The Doklam standoff, at the Sikkim-Bhutan-Tibet trijunction just west of this sector, shows the 1890 line is still read differently by China and by India and Bhutan at its southern end.', ['h_doklam']),
)
